Temperature Limits and Safety Considerations for Gas
Cylinders in a Heated Cabinet (A)- D2.036
Gaurav
May 27, 2026
1 Introduction
This document summarises the temperature limits, pressure behaviour, and safety considerations
for several compressed gas and refrigerant cylinders intended for storage inside a heated cabinet.
The purpose of this report is to assist with engineering and safety planning for cabinet heating
systems where gas cylinders may be exposed to elevated temperatures.
2 General Safety Guidance
For nearly all industrial gas and refrigerant cylinders, the commonly accepted maximum external
cylinder temperature is:
Tmax ≈ 50–52 ◦C
This limit is widely used by industrial gas suppliers and refrigerant manufacturers.
The reasons for this limit include:
• Rapid increase of internal pressure with temperature.
• Possible activation of pressure relief devices.
• Risk of cylinder rupture if severely overheated.
• Increased fire and explosion hazards for flammable gases.
• Increased toxicity risks from decomposition products in refrigerants.
2.1 Important Cabinet Design Recommendations
• Cabinet air temperature should normally remain below 45◦C to maintain engineering
margin.
• Local heater hot-spots must not directly irradiate cylinder walls.
• Forced-air circulation is strongly recommended.
• Independent over-temperature protection should be installed.
• Continuous temperature monitoring is recommended.
• Ventilation is required for all flammable gases and refrigerants.
