4 Detailed Gas Information
4.1 R134a (UN3159)
Chemical name:
1,1,1,2-Tetrafluoroethane
R134a is a non-flammable HFC refrigerant stored as a liquefied gas.
Approximate saturation pressures:
• 6bar at 20◦C
• 13bar at 50◦C
Primary risks:
• Rapid pressure increase during heating.
• Pressure relief valve activation.
• Toxic hydrogen fluoride generation during fire decomposition.
4.2 SF6 (UN1080)
Sulfur hexafluoride is a dense inert insulating gas stored at high pressure.
Approximate pressures:
• 20.7bar at 20◦C
2
• Approximately 35bar to 40bar near 50◦C
Primary risks:
• High-pressure stored energy.
• Oxygen displacement in confined spaces.
• Toxic decomposition products after electrical arcing.
4.3 Neon (UN1065)
Neon is an inert noble gas stored as a compressed gas.
Typical cylinder conditions:
• Fill pressure typically 200bar to 230bar
• Hydrostatic test pressure commonly above 300bar
Estimated pressure increase:
• Approximately 200bar at 15◦C
• Approximately 225bar at 50◦C
Primary risks:
• Large stored mechanical energy.
• High-velocity gas release if damaged.
• Asphyxiation risk.
4.4 Isobutane (UN1969)
Isobutane is a liquefied flammable hydrocarbon gas.
Approximate vapour pressures:
• 3.1bar at 20◦C
• 5.3bar at 40◦C
• 6.9bar at 50◦C
Primary risks:
• Fire and explosion.
• Flammable vent release.
• BLEVE-type failure during severe overheating.
Special precautions:
• Ventilation is mandatory.
• Ignition sources must be eliminated.
• Heater elements must be isolated from leaked gas.
